SECTION 5 — API CONFIGURATION
═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

The user must set up their own API keys. Luna supports two providers.
Multiple API keys per provider can be saved and named.

Supported providers:
1. Google Gemini (api.generativeai.google.com)
2. OpenRouter (openrouter.ai/api/v1)

API key management features:
- Add a new key with a custom label (e.g., "My Gemini Key 1")
- Save multiple keys per provider
- Set one key as active per provider
- Test connection before saving (real HTTP test request)
- Show/hide key (masked by default)
- Edit key label
- Delete a key (with confirmation)
- Select active provider (Gemini or OpenRouter)
- If active key fails, optionally auto-rotate to next available key

Storage of keys:
- Store in EncryptedSharedPreferences or DataStore with encryption
- Never log or print keys
- Never hardcode any key
- Explain to the user that client-side key storage has security limits
- Recommend using the app only on personal devices

Connection test logic:
- Send a minimal test message like "Hello"
- Display real response or real error
- Never fabricate a successful connection
- Error states: missing key, invalid key, quota exceeded,
  network unavailable, model unsupported, server error

API call logic:
- Use active provider's active key for all requests
- Include full conversation context in every request (multi-turn memory)
- Support streaming responses (show text as it arrives)
- Handle rate limits gracefully with retry logic
- Max context window: trim oldest messages if context exceeds limit

Models:
Gemini: gemini-1.5-flash (default), gemini-1.5-pro (optional)
OpenRouter: user selects from available free/paid models
            show a model picker populated from OpenRouter's model list API

